WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.510
On the board I wrote: “The bus didn’t

2
00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:06.230
stop at the school because it was…”

3
00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:09.850
My pupils called out “full” and “late”.

4
00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:11.940
A model does the same.

5
00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:14.910
But how does it know what “it” means?

6
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:18.320
Today: inside a language

7
00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:19.800
model, step by step.

8
00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:22.080
You know the first step:

9
00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:24.480
text is cut into tokens.

10
00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:27.870
Each token becomes a long list of

11
00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:29.510
numbers, learned in training.

12
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:32.540
Words used in similar ways get

13
00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:36.060
similar lists, so “bus” sits close

14
00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:38.740
to “matatu”, far from “mango”.

15
00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:41.190
Next, attention.

16
00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:44.600
As the model writes, the newest token

17
00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.380
looks back at every earlier token and

18
00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:48.660
weighs which ones matter.

19
00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:52.000
Here, “bus” and “stop” matter a

20
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.360
lot; “at” hardly at all.

21
00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:56.380
That mix becomes the

22
00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:58.020
context for the next word.

23
00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:01.970
Then layers of neurons, like those in

24
00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:03.650
lesson two, work on the numbers.

25
00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:06.270
Attention and neurons take

26
00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:08.130
turns, dozens of times.

27
00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:11.870
At the top, it scores every possible next

28
00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:14.470
token, picks one, and starts again.

29
00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:18.630
So a language model is a huge neural

30
00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:21.510
network trained to predict the next token.

31
00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:24.990
Its loop: tokens, numbers,

32
00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:28.810
attention, scores, pick, repeat.

33
00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:31.690
Where do its billions of

34
00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:32.570
weights come from?

35
00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:34.650
Training comes in stages,

36
00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:35.970
like a teacher’s.

37
00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:38.640
Pretraining, like years of

38
00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:40.960
reading: predicting the next token

39
00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:42.920
across huge amounts of text.

40
00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:46.310
Then fine-tuning, like teaching

41
00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:48.770
practice: examples of good answers,

42
00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:51.110
and people rating which is better.

43
00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:52.830
Quick check!

44
00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:56.820
When the model writes “full”, which step

45
00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:58.720
lets it use “bus” from earlier?

46
00:02:00.010 --> 00:02:00.330
Attention.

47
00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:03.650
It lets the newest token draw on

48
00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:06.310
“bus” and “stop”, further back.

49
00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:09.380
Without it, the model would see little

50
00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:12.340
more than “was”, and could guess anything.

51
00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:15.500
Notice what the loop never

52
00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:17.660
does: check a fact.

53
00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:20.010
It learned which text is

54
00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:22.490
likely, not which is true.

55
00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:25.930
A rare fact, like a local history date,

56
00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:28.530
can come out wrong, just as smoothly.

57
00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:32.080
And if training rewards any answer over

58
00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:34.440
“I don’t know”, it learns to guess.

59
00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:36.820
Two habits help.

60
00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:39.860
Grounding: give it a trusted source,

61
00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:42.580
like the textbook page, and ask it

62
00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:44.820
to answer only from that, so

63
00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:46.360
attention draws on the page.

64
00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:49.620
Then check against the source: grounding

65
00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:52.320
lowers mistakes, but doesn’t remove them.

66
00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:56.290
In the lab, a tiny model trained on

67
00:02:56.290 --> 00:02:59.510
twenty-four sentences writes for you.

68
00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:02.350
Start with “The teacher”, press

69
00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:05.210
Next token, watch each step.

70
00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:07.000
Then make it adventurous.

71
00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:09.440
Your turn.

72
00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:12.720
Why can a language model sound

73
00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:14.540
sure and still be wrong?

74
00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:18.040
It predicts likely text; it

75
00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:19.020
doesn’t check facts.

76
00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:22.730
Remember the loop: tokens, numbers,

77
00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:26.550
attention, scores, pick, repeat.

78
00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:29.410
It’s pretrained on huge amounts

79
00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:31.490
of text, then fine-tuned.

80
00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.020
So ground it, then check.

81
00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:36.700
Try it in class tomorrow:

82
00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:38.360
play “Who is ‘it’?”.

83
00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:41.650
Write “The goat ate the maize

84
00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:42.830
because it was hungry.”

85
00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:46.160
Pupils draw arrows from “it” to

86
00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:47.260
the words that give it away.

87
00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:49.850
Then change “hungry” to

88
00:03:49.850 --> 00:03:52.050
“sweet”: the arrows move.
